1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
The scrapyard behind the train station.

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,540
Nia lands, Kai climbs the fence, and you

3
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:05,920
walk in through the gate like a normal

4
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:06,260
person.

5
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:08,340
On top of a pile of old cars

6
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,540
sits a boy with headphones.

7
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:10,920
His name is Ravi.

8
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:12,080
He doesn't get up.

9
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:13,380
He doesn't even look at you.

10
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:14,660
He just lifts one finger.

11
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,840
And the car underneath him starts to float.

12
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:19,280
So what if you could move things just

13
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:19,880
by thinking?

14
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:21,180
No hands.

15
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:22,300
No strings.

16
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,520
First, physics wants to talk about the bill.

17
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:28,510
Lifting a car two metres takes about thirty

18
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:30,000
thousand joules of energy.

19
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,160
Physics doesn't care if you use your arms,

20
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:33,860
a crane, or your brain.

21
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,600
And your brain runs on about twenty watts.

22
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:38,060
Less than an old light bulb.

23
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:39,980
If all of it went into lifting, it

24
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:42,380
would take about twenty-five minutes to raise that

25
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:42,640
car.

26
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,580
And you'd have nothing left for thinking.

27
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:47,220
Ravi does it in two seconds.

28
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:49,400
So his power isn't coming from his brain.

29
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:51,540
Nobody knows where it comes from.

30
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:52,960
Not even Ravi.

31
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,260
Here's the second problem.

32
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:57,360
Every push pushes back.

33
00:00:57,720 --> 00:00:58,840
That's Newton's third law.

34
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:00,960
When Ravi shoves a car across the yard,

35
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:02,860
something has to push him the other way.

36
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,660
That's why he sits down to do it.

37
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:06,220
Standing up, he'd go flying.

38
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,700
Now the fun part.

39
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:11,360
Humans can already move things without touching them.

40
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:12,620
Just not with our minds.

41
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:15,780
In 1997, in the Netherlands, a physicist named

42
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:18,500
Andre Geim put a tiny live frog inside

43
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:20,200
a very powerful magnet.

44
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:22,660
Hundreds of thousands of times stronger than the

45
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:23,440
Earth's magnetic field.

46
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:26,100
The frog floated in mid-air.

47
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:26,620
Unharmed.

48
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:29,080
A magnet that strong pushes on everything a

49
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:29,560
tiny bit.

50
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:30,380
Even water.

51
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:31,220
Even frogs.

52
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:32,000
Even you.

53
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,260
Geim won a joke prize for it, the

54
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:34,820
Ig Nobel.

55
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,080
Ten years later, he won the real Nobel

56
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:38,180
Prize, for something else.

57
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:40,260
He's the only person who has won both.

58
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:42,840
And light can push too.

59
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:46,400
Arthur Ashkin built optical tweezers: a laser beam

60
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:48,180
that can grab and hold living bacteria.

61
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,780
In 2018, at ninety-six, he won the Nobel

62
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:52,540
Prize for it.

63
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:54,860
And moving things with your mind?

64
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:56,080
That happened too.

65
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,420
In 2012, a woman named Cathy Hutchinson, paralysed

66
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:01,920
by a stroke, had a tiny sensor placed

67
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:02,580
in her brain.

68
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,380
She imagined picking up a cup of coffee.

69
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:06,860
A robot arm did it.

70
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:09,220
Her first sip on her own in about

71
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:10,260
fifteen years.

72
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:11,600
Not magic.

73
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:14,140
A sensor, some wires, and a computer.

74
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:16,460
But she moved something with a thought.

75
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:19,520
That night, Ravi walks with you along the

76
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:19,700
river.

77
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,300
He skips stones across the water without throwing

78
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:22,540
them.

79
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,020
Nia points at the sky.

80
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:26,760
The star that isn't a star is there

81
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:27,140
again.

82
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:27,700
Brighter.

83
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:30,400
Ravi looks up, and for the first time,

84
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:31,720
he doesn't look bored.

85
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:33,720
Can you push that?

86
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:34,520
Kai asks.

87
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:35,860
Ravi grins.

88
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:37,040
Not yet.

89
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:40,140
So what's the first thing you'd move?

90
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:41,280
Tell us in the comments.

91
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,120
And subscribe, because next time, you meet the

92
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:46,260
strongest person in the group, and find out

93
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:47,960
why super strength would break your bones.
